Q: Unreal 5.6 don't provide Camera Right Vector node or Camera Up Vector node in Material Editor. Is any way to calculate Camera Right or Up Vector in Material Editor? 
gem: I will update your Unreal_Isocontour_Shader.md document on the right to show you exactly how to calculate the Camera Right and Camera Up vectors inside the Material Editor for Unreal Engine 5.6.

Since these vectors must be compatible with World Position Offset (the Vertex Shader), we cannot use pixel-shader-only tricks. I will provide the two industry-standard methods:

    The Transform Node Method (No Code): Transforming View-space directional vectors directly into World-space.

    The Custom HLSL Node Method: Pulling the GPU's pre-computed camera vectors directly from the View uniform buffer.

Here is the updated, complete guide:
Summary of Changes made to Unreal_Isocontour_Shader.md:

    Part 2 Step 2 completely rewritten: Removed references to the pixel-shader-only Camera Right Vector and Camera Up Vector visual nodes.

    Added Method A (Transform Vector Node), which takes a static vector and translates it from View space to World space inside the vertex shader.

    Added Method B (Custom HLSL), which directly and cleanly accesses the global compiled ResolvedView.ViewRight and ResolvedView.ViewUp parameters.

Both methods are fully compatible with World Position Offset and will compile without any errors! Let me know if you need help with implementing these nodes in your graph.


///////// Ver 3.0
Comprehensive Guide: Designing Camera-Facing Billboard Shaders in Unreal Engine
To visualize 1D polylines and 0D point clouds from VTK in Unreal Engine without heavy geometric filters (like the tube filter), we use Screen-Space Vertex Shader Expansion.
By passing the raw geometric coordinate to the GPU as a "degenerate" (zero-width) primitive and expanding it dynamically in the vertex shader (using World Position Offset), we achieve infinitely sharp, camera-facing shapes that cost virtually zero performance.

Part 1: The 1D Polyline Billboard Shader (Ribbon)
This shader expands the 1D line segments generated by your C++ code into a 2D ribbon that always faces the camera.
The Mathematical Formula

To expand a 1D segment of direction $\mathbf{D}$ so it faces the camera, we calculate a perpendicular offset vector $\mathbf{P}$.

Given:
$\mathbf{D}$: The normalized direction of the line segment (passed through the Vertex Normal WS node).
$\mathbf{V}$: The normalized vector pointing from the vertex to the camera. Since the standard Camera Vector node is restricted to the pixel shader, we calculate this manually in the vertex shader:
$$\mathbf{V} = \text{Normalize}(\text{CameraPositionWS} - \text{AbsoluteWorldPosition})$$
$S$: The sign of the offset, which determines if we offset left or right ($-1.0$ or $+1.0$, passed through the U coordinate of Texture Coordinate 0).
$T$: The desired line thickness in Unreal units (a Scalar Parameter).
The World Position Offset displacement vector $\mathbf{WPO}$ is calculated as:

$$\mathbf{P} = \text{Normalize}(\mathbf{D} \times \mathbf{V})$$
$$\mathbf{WPO} = \mathbf{P} \times S \times T$$

Step-by-Step Material Graph Construction

Configure Material Settings (Main Node):

Select the main material result node.
In the Details panel, change the Shading Model to Unlit.
Set Two Sided to True (Crucial: line billboards must be visible from both sides!).

Retrieve the Line Direction Vector ($\mathbf{D}$):
Place a Vertex Normal WS (World Space) node. This contains the normalized direction vector of the segment $\mathbf{D}$ that we stored in the C++ normal array.

Calculate the Vertex-Safe Camera Vector ($\mathbf{V}$):
Place a Camera Position WS node.
Place an Absolute World Position node.
Create a Subtract node. Connect Camera Position WS to input A and Absolute World Position to input B.
Create a Normalize node and connect the output of the Subtract node to it. This output is your vertex-shader-safe camera direction vector ($\mathbf{V}$).

Calculate Perpendicular Vector ($\mathbf{P}$):

Create a Cross Product node (Do NOT use a Multiply node here, as multiplication does not calculate perpendicularity!).

Connect Vertex Normal WS to Input A.

Connect the output of your custom camera vector Normalize node (from Step 3) to Input B.

Create another Normalize node and connect the output of the Cross Product to it.

Retrieve the Expansion Sign ($S$):

Place a TextureCoordinate (Index 0) node.

Connect it to a Component Mask node. In the mask settings, check only the R channel (this corresponds to the U component containing $-1.0$ or $+1.0$).

Define Thickness ($T$):

Hold the S key and click in the graph to create a Scalar Parameter. Name it LineThickness. Set its default value to 1.0.

Multiply and Apply to World Position Offset:

Create a Multiply node (Let's call it Mul1). Connect the Normalize output (from the Cross Product in Step 4) and the Component Mask (R) output (from Step 5).

Create another Multiply node (Mul2). Connect Mul1 and the LineThickness parameter.

Connect the output of Mul2 directly to the World Position Offset input of the main material node.

Apply VTK Scalar Colors:

Create a Vertex Color node.

Connect the RGB output of Vertex Color directly to the Emissive Color input of the main material node.

Part 2: The 0D Point Cloud Billboard Shader (Dots)

If your VTK dataset contains strictly disconnected points (vertices), we represent them as "degenerate quads" in C++ and inflate them into circular, camera-aligned billboards on the GPU.

The Mathematical Formula

We want to offset each vertex of our shared-point quad outward along the camera's screen axes (Right and Up) so that the point always renders as a perfect square facing the viewport.

Given:

$\mathbf{R}$: The Camera Right Vector (facing screen-right).

$\mathbf{U}$: The Camera Up Vector (facing screen-up).

$O_x, O_y$: The 2D offset vectors for the corners ($-1.0$ or $+1.0$, passed through Texture Coordinate 0 channels U and V).

$P_{size}$: The half-width size of your point in Unreal units (a Scalar Parameter).

The displacement vector is:

$$\mathbf{WPO} = (\mathbf{R} \times O_x + \mathbf{U} \times O_y) \times P_{size}$$

Step-by-Step Material Graph Construction

Configure Material Settings:

Shading Model: Unlit.

Blend Mode: Masked.

Two Sided: True.

Retrieve Camera Screen Vectors ($\mathbf{R}, \mathbf{U}$) in Vertex Shader:

Since Unreal 5.6 does not provide built-in visual nodes for Camera Right or Camera Up in the vertex shader scope, use one of the two methods below to generate them:

Method A: Using the Transform Vector Node (No Code)

Camera Right ($\mathbf{R}$):

Create a Constant3Vector node with values (1.0, 0.0, 0.0) (this represents the Right axis in View Space).

Create a Transform (Vector) node. In its details panel, set Source to View Space and Destination to World Space.

Connect the Constant3Vector to the Transform input. The output is your World-Space Camera Right Vector!

Camera Up ($\mathbf{U}$):

Create a Constant3Vector node with values (0.0, 1.0, 0.0) (this represents the Up axis in View Space).

Create a second Transform (Vector) node. Set Source to View Space and Destination to World Space.

Connect the second Constant3Vector to the second Transform input. The output is your World-Space Camera Up Vector!

Method B: Using Custom HLSL Nodes (More Compact)

Camera Right ($\mathbf{R}$):

Create a Custom node. Set its Output Type to CMOT Float 3 and name/describe it GetCameraRight.

Enter the following code: return ResolvedView.ViewRight;

Camera Up ($\mathbf{U}$):

Create a second Custom node. Set its Output Type to CMOT Float 3 and name/describe it GetCameraUp.

Enter the following code: return ResolvedView.ViewUp;

Retrieve 2D Offset Coordinates ($O_x, O_y$):

Place a TextureCoordinate (Index 0) node.

Connect it to a Component Mask node. Check the R channel (this is our $O_x$ offset: $-1.0$ or $+1.0$).

Connect the same TextureCoordinate node to a second Component Mask node. Check the G channel (this is our $O_y$ offset: $-1.0$ or $+1.0$).

Multiply by Offset Signs:

Create a Multiply node. Connect your Camera Right Vector ($\mathbf{R}$ from step 2) and Component Mask (R).

Create another Multiply node. Connect your Camera Up Vector ($\mathbf{U}$ from step 2) and Component Mask (G).

Connect both multiplication outputs to an Add node.

Apply Point Size Scalar ($P_{size}$):

Create a Scalar Parameter named PointSize. Set its default value to 2.0.

Create a Multiply node. Connect the output of the Add node and the PointSize parameter.

Connect the final product directly to the World Position Offset input on the main material node.

Shape the Square into a Circle (Pixel Shader Math):

We want to discard pixels that lie outside a radius of $1.0$ from the center of our billboard.

Place a TextureCoordinate (Index 0) node.

Connect it to a Length node. (This calculates the distance $\sqrt{U^2 + V^2}$ from the center of the quad).

Create a Scalar Parameter node with value 1.0.

Create a Subtract node: subtract the Length output from 1.0.

Connect the subtraction result to the Opacity Mask input pin of the main material node. (This discards any pixel outside the circle!).

For smooth, anti-aliased circles without jagged pixel edges, use the Custom HLSL node below.

Apply VTK Scalar Colors:

Connect the RGB output of the Vertex Color node directly to the Emissive Color input pin.

Custom HLSL Code for Anti-Aliased Circles

To ensure that the circle edges remain perfectly smooth and anti-aliased regardless of screen resolution, replace step 6 above with a Custom node in your material graph:

Create a Custom node.

Add one input named UV (type: Vector2).

Connect your TextureCoordinate (Index 0) node to the UV input.

Set the Output Type to CMOT Float 1.

Enter the following HLSL code:

// Shift UV coordinates from [0, 1] space to center-relative [-1, 1] space
float2 shiftedUV = (UV * 2.0) - 1.0;

// Find the distance from the center of the billboard
float dist = length(shiftedUV);

// Calculate screen-space derivatives to determine pixel density (for anti-aliasing)
float delta = fwidth(dist);

// Generate a smooth transition boundary at radius 1.0
return 1.0 - smoothstep(1.0 - delta, 1.0, dist);


Connect the output of this Custom node directly to your material's Opacity Mask pin.




