Comprehensive Guide: Designing Camera-Facing Billboard Shaders in Unreal Engine
To visualize 1D polylines and 0D point clouds from VTK in Unreal Engine without heavy geometric filters (like the tube filter), we use Screen-Space Vertex Shader Expansion.
By passing the raw geometric coordinate to the GPU as a "degenerate" (zero-width) primitive and expanding it dynamically in the vertex shader (using World Position Offset), we achieve infinitely sharp, camera-facing shapes that cost virtually zero performance.

///////////////////////////////////////////
Part 1: The 1D Polyline Billboard Shader (Ribbon)

This shader expands the 1D line segments generated by your C++ code into a 2D ribbon that always faces the camera.
The Mathematical Formula

To expand a 1D segment of direction $\mathbf{D}$ so it faces the camera, we calculate a perpendicular offset vector $\mathbf{P}$.

Given:
$\mathbf{D}$: The normalized direction of the line segment (passed through the Vertex Normal node).
$\mathbf{V}$: The normalized vector pointing from the pixel/vertex to the camera (passed through the Camera Vector node).
$S$: The sign of the offset, which determines if we offset left or right ($-1.0$ or $+1.0$, passed through the U coordinate of Texture Coordinate 0).
$T$: The desired line thickness in Unreal units (a Scalar Parameter).

The World Position Offset displacement vector $\mathbf{WPO}$ is calculated as:

$$\mathbf{P} = \text{Normalize}(\mathbf{D} \times \mathbf{V})$$
$$\mathbf{WPO} = \mathbf{P} \times S \times T$$

Step-by-Step Material Graph Construction

Configure Material Settings (Main Node):

Select the main material result node.

In the Details panel, change the Shading Model to Unlit.

Set Two Sided to True (Crucial: line billboards must be visible from both sides!).

Retrieve the Line Direction Vector ($\mathbf{D}$):

Place a Vertex Normal WS (World Space) node. This contains the normalized direction vector of the segment $\mathbf{D}$ that we stored in the C++ normal array.

Retrieve the Camera Vector ($\mathbf{V}$):

Place a Camera Vector WS node. This represents the normalized vector pointing from the vertex to the camera position.

Calculate Perpendicular Vector ($\mathbf{P}$):

Create a Cross Product node.

Connect Vertex Normal WS to Input A.

Connect Camera Vector WS to Input B.

Create a Normalize node and connect the output of the Cross Product to it.

Retrieve the Expansion Sign ($S$):

Place a TextureCoordinate (Index 0) node.

Connect it to a Component Mask node. In the mask settings, check only the R channel (this corresponds to the U component containing $-1.0$ or $+1.0$).

Define Thickness ($T$):

Hold the S key and click in the graph to create a Scalar Parameter. Name it LineThickness. Set its default value to 1.0 (adjust based on your scale).

Multiply and Apply to World Position Offset:

Create a Multiply node (Let's call it Mul1). Connect the Normalize output and the Component Mask (R) output.

Create another Multiply node (Mul2). Connect Mul1 and the LineThickness parameter.

Connect the output of Mul2 directly to the World Position Offset input of the main material node.

Apply VTK Scalar Colors:

Create a Vertex Color node.

Connect the RGB output of Vertex Color directly to the Emissive Color input of the main material node.

