Part 2: The 0D Point Cloud Billboard Shader (Dots)
If your VTK dataset contains strictly disconnected points (vertices), we represent them as "degenerate quads" in C++ and inflate them into circular, camera-aligned billboards on the GPU.
The Mathematical Formula
We want to offset each vertex of our shared-point quad outward along the camera's screen axes (Right and Up) so that the point always renders as a perfect square facing the viewport.

Given:
$\mathbf{R}$: The Camera Right Vector (facing screen-right).

$\mathbf{U}$: The Camera Up Vector (facing screen-up).

$O_x, O_y$: The 2D offset vectors for the corners ($-1.0$ or $+1.0$, passed through Texture Coordinate 0 channels U and V).

$P_{size}$: The half-width size of your point in Unreal units (a Scalar Parameter).

The displacement vector is:


$$\mathbf{WPO} = (\mathbf{R} \times O_x + \mathbf{U} \times O_y) \times P_{size}$$

Step-by-Step Material Graph Construction

Configure Material Settings:

Shading Model: Unlit.

Blend Mode: Masked.

Two Sided: True.

Retrieve Camera Screen Vectors ($\mathbf{R}, \mathbf{U}$):

Place a Camera Right Vector node.

Place a Camera Up Vector node.

Retrieve 2D Offset Coordinates ($O_x, O_y$):

Place a TextureCoordinate (Index 0) node.

Connect it to a Component Mask node. Check the R channel (this is our $O_x$ offset: $-1.0$ or $+1.0$).

Connect the same TextureCoordinate node to a second Component Mask node. Check the G channel (this is our $O_y$ offset: $-1.0$ or $+1.0$).

Multiply by Offset Signs:

Create a Multiply node. Connect Camera Right Vector and Component Mask (R).

Create another Multiply node. Connect Camera Up Vector and Component Mask (G).

Connect both multiplication outputs to an Add node.

Apply Point Size Scalar ($P_{size}$):

Create a Scalar Parameter named PointSize. Set its default value to 2.0.

Create a Multiply node. Connect the output of the Add node and the PointSize parameter.

Connect the final product directly to the World Position Offset input on the main material node.

Shape the Square into a Circle (Pixel Shader Math):

We want to discard pixels that lie outside a radius of $1.0$ from the center of our billboard.

Place a TextureCoordinate (Index 0) node.

Connect it to a Length node. (This calculates the distance $\sqrt{U^2 + V^2}$ from the center of the quad).

Create a Scalar Parameter node with value 1.0.

Create a Subtract node: subtract the Length output from 1.0.

Connect the subtraction result to the Opacity Mask input pin of the main material node. (This discards any pixel outside the circle!).

For smooth, anti-aliased circles without jagged pixel edges, use the Custom HLSL node below.

Apply VTK Scalar Colors:

Connect the RGB output of the Vertex Color node directly to the Emissive Color input pin.

Custom HLSL Code for Anti-Aliased Circles

To ensure that the circle edges remain perfectly smooth and anti-aliased regardless of screen resolution, replace step 6 above with a Custom node in your material graph:

Create a Custom node.

Add one input named UV (type: Vector2).

Connect your TextureCoordinate (Index 0) node to the UV input.

Set the Output Type to CMOT Float 1.

Enter the following HLSL code:

// Shift UV coordinates from [0, 1] space to center-relative [-1, 1] space
float2 shiftedUV = (UV * 2.0) - 1.0;

// Find the distance from the center of the billboard
float dist = length(shiftedUV);

// Calculate screen-space derivatives to determine pixel density (for anti-aliasing)
float delta = fwidth(dist);

// Generate a smooth transition boundary at radius 1.0
return 1.0 - smoothstep(1.0 - delta, 1.0, dist);


Connect the output of this Custom node directly to your material's Opacity Mask pin.